## Why This Is Asked
Practical beginner task validating end-to-end ingestion, data quality, and cataloging.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event-driven ETL
- Glue Spark/DynamicFrame validation
- Quarantine routing for bad records
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog and Athena access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo Glue-like JS snippet for validation
function process(batch){
  const valid=[], quarantined=[];
  for (const r of batch){
    if (r.a != null && r.b != null) valid.push(transform(r));
    else quarantined.push({row:r, validation_reason:'missing_required_fields'});
  }
  writeParquet(valid, datePartition);
  writeJson(quarantined, quarantineBucket);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution if new fields are added?
- How would you monitor the quarantine rate and alert if it spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly understanding of end-to-end data ingestion with a realistic cost-sensitive constraint. It also introduces basic data quality and idempotency patterns not covered in prior questions.

## Key Concepts
- Ingestion pipelines with Glue Studio
- Parquet writing and date partitioning
- Idempotent loads via load_id/marker files
- Basic data quality checks (row count stability, distinct count)
- Cost monitoring with CloudWatch and Budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent marker example (conceptual)
const markerPath = `s3://corp-logs/markers/logs_${yyyymmdd}.marker`;
if (!exists(markerPath)) {
  // run load, then write marker
  mark(markerPath);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
